\kerning0\cf0\ftnbj\fet2\ftnstart1\ftnnar\aftnnar\ftnstart1\aftnstart1\aenddoc\revprop3{\*\rdf}{\info\uc1}\deftab720\viewkind1\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1440\margr1440\widowctrl
\sectd\sbknone\colsx360\headery0\footery0\pgncont\ltrsect
\pard\plain\ltrpar\qc\sb2880\sa480\itap0{\f0\fs36\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Mathematical Validation of the Converse Prime Gatekeeper Filter}{\f0\fs36\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\qc\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs28\i\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch A Rigorous Proof from First Principles}{\f0\fs28\i\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\qc\sa1440\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch February 2, 2026}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\qc\sb720\itap0{\f0\fs24\i\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Validating 25 Years of Research into Converse Primes}{\f0\fs24\i\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch \page }{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa240\s21\itap0{\s21\f0\fs32\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Abstract}{\s21\f0\fs32\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch This document presents a rigorous mathematical proof validating the filtering constraints used in a 25-year investigation into converse primes\uc1\u8212\'97extraordinarily rare mathematical objects where both a prime p and its digit reversal are expressible as sums of consecutive squares (n\'b2 + (n+1)\'b2). Through computational analysis of the modular arithmetic cycle of 2n\'b2 + 2n + 1, we prove that the empirically-derived 'gatekeeper filter' is not a heuristic but a mathematical necessity arising from the fundamental structure of the generating formula.}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Key findings: (1) The last-two-digit cycle has period 25 with exactly 6 valid patterns satisfying necessary prime constraints. (2) The emirp symmetry constraint mathematically derives exactly 6 valid first-two-digit patterns. (3) Dead zones in the search space (e.g., 6.94\'d710\'b2\uc0\u8308  to 10\'b2\uc0\u8309 ) are provably devoid of candidates. (4) The discovery of the unique converse pair 12641 \uc0\u8596  14621 is validated within this rigorous mathematical framework.}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa240\s21\itap0{\s21\f0\fs32\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 1. Introduction}{\s21\f0\fs32\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb180\sa180\s22\itap0{\s22\f0\fs28\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 1.1 Historical Context}{\s22\f0\fs28\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch In the 1990s, a computational search discovered an apparently unique mathematical object: the converse prime pair 12641 \uc0\u8596  14621. This pair possesses the remarkable property that both numbers and their digit reversals are simultaneously:}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
{\listtext\pard\fi-360\li720\bullet 	}\pard\plain{\ltrpar\ql\fi-360\li720\s30{\*\abilist\abilistid11200\abilistparentid0\abilistlevel1\abistartat1{\abifieldfont NULL}{\abilistdecimal .}{\abilistdelim %L.}{\abiliststyle None}}{\*\pn\pnql\pnstart1\pnlvlblt{\pntxtb\bullet}}\ls1\ilvl0\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Prime numbers}{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
}{\listtext\pard\fi-360\li720\bullet 	}\pard\plain{\ltrpar\ql\fi-360\li720\s30{\*\abilist\abilistid11200\abilistparentid0\abilistlevel1\abistartat1{\abifieldfont NULL}{\abilistdecimal .}{\abilistdelim %L.}{\abiliststyle None}}{\*\pn\pnql\pnstart1\pnlvlblt{\pntxtb\bullet}}\ls1\ilvl0\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Emirps (reversible primes where both p and reverse(p) are prime)}{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
}{\listtext\pard\fi-360\li720\bullet 	}\pard\plain{\ltrpar\ql\fi-360\li720\s30{\*\abilist\abilistid11200\abilistparentid0\abilistlevel1\abistartat1{\abifieldfont NULL}{\abilistdecimal .}{\abilistdelim %L.}{\abiliststyle None}}{\*\pn\pnql\pnstart1\pnlvlblt{\pntxtb\bullet}}\ls1\ilvl0\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Expressible as sums of consecutive squares: n\'b2 + (n+1)\'b2}{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa240\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Over 25 years of systematic computational searches, testing ranges up to 10\'b2\uc0\u8308 , have found no additional converse pairs. The search methodology employed a 'gatekeeper filter' based on observed patterns in candidate generation, but until now, the mathematical rigor of this filter remained unproven.}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb180\sa180\s22\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 1.2 The Discovery Context}{\s22\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch On February 1-2, 2026, a distributed computation across two machines (nitroIII and nitroII) attempted to search the range 10\'b2\uc0\u8308  to 10\'b2\uc0\u8309 . The nitroIII machine successfully generated 2.5 TB of candidate data, while nitroII\uc1\u8212\'97assigned the upper 33% of the range (6.94\'d710\'b2\uc0\u8308  to 10\'b2\uc0\u8309 )\uc1\u8212\'97ran for over 4 hours and generated zero candidates. This 'failure' prompted a fundamental investigation into whether the gatekeeper filter was incorrectly rejecting valid candidates.}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch The investigation revealed that the failure was not a bug but a mathematical truth: the upper range is a dead zone where no valid candidates can exist. This discovery led to a rigorous proof of the gatekeeper constraints from first principles.}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa240\s21\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 2. The Mathematical Framework}{\s21\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb180\sa180\s22\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 2.1 The Generating Formula}{\s22\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch The sum of two consecutive squares can be expressed algebraically as:}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\qc\sb240\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs28\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch n\'b2 + (n+1)\'b2 = 2n\'b2 + 2n + 1}{\f0\fs28\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch This algebraic form is the foundation for all subsequent analysis. Every prime p in our search must satisfy p = 2n\'b2 + 2n + 1 for some positive integer n.}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb180\sa180\s22\itap0{\s22\f0\fs28\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 2.2 Necessary Conditions}{\s22\f0\fs28\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch For a number from this formula to potentially be prime, it must satisfy certain modular arithmetic constraints. Most fundamentally, examining the form 2n\'b2 + 2n + 1 modulo 4:}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\qc\sb240\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs26\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 2n\'b2 + 2n + 1 \uc0\u8801  2n(n+1) + 1 \uc0\u8801  1 (mod 4)}{\f0\fs26\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch This holds because n(n+1) is always even (product of consecutive integers), making 2n(n+1) \uc0\u8801  0 (mod 4). Therefore, all numbers from this formula are congruent to 1 modulo 4. This is a necessary but not sufficient condition for primality.}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa240\s21\itap0{\s21\f0\fs32\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 3. The Core Proof: Last-Two-Digits Cycle}{\s21\f0\fs32\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb180\sa180\s22\itap0{\s22\f0\fs28\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 3.1 Computational Analysis}{\s22\f0\fs28\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch To determine which last-two-digit patterns are mathematically possible from the formula 2n\'b2 + 2n + 1, we computed the cycle modulo 100. The computation revealed:}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
{\listtext\pard\fi-360\li720\bullet 	}\pard\plain{\ltrpar\ql\fi-360\li720\s30{\*\abilist\abilistid11200\abilistparentid0\abilistlevel1\abistartat1{\abifieldfont NULL}{\abilistdecimal .}{\abilistdelim %L.}{\abiliststyle None}}{\*\pn\pnql\pnstart1\pnlvlblt{\pntxtb\bullet}}\ls1\ilvl0\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch The cycle has period 25 (repeats every 25 values of n)}{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
}{\listtext\pard\fi-360\li720\bullet 	}\pard\plain{\ltrpar\ql\fi-360\li720\s30{\*\abilist\abilistid11200\abilistparentid0\abilistlevel1\abistartat1{\abifieldfont NULL}{\abilistdecimal .}{\abilistdelim %L.}{\abiliststyle None}}{\*\pn\pnql\pnstart1\pnlvlblt{\pntxtb\bullet}}\ls1\ilvl0\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Only 11 distinct values appear in the cycle: 01, 05, 13, 21, 25, 41, 45, 61, 65, 81, 85}{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
}{\listtext\pard\fi-360\li720\bullet 	}\pard\plain{\ltrpar\ql\fi-360\li720\s30{\*\abilist\abilistid11200\abilistparentid0\abilistlevel1\abistartat1{\abifieldfont NULL}{\abilistdecimal .}{\abilistdelim %L.}{\abiliststyle None}}{\*\pn\pnql\pnstart1\pnlvlblt{\pntxtb\bullet}}\ls1\ilvl0\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Filtering for values \uc0\u8801  1 (mod 4) that end in 1 or 3 yields exactly 6 patterns}{\s30\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
}\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb180\sa180\s22\itap0{\s30\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 3.2 The Six Valid Patterns}{\s22\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch The complete set of mathematically possible last-two-digit patterns is:}{\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\qc\sb240\sa240\itap0{\cf3\f0\fs32\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 01, 13, 21, 41, 61, 81}{\cf3\f0\fs32\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Critically, patterns such as 33, 53, 73, and 93\uc1\u8212\'97which might appear to satisfy mod 4 constraints\uc1\u8212\'97do NOT appear in the cycle and therefore CANNOT be generated by the formula 2n\'b2 + 2n + 1. This is not an empirical observation but a mathematical certainty proven by cycle analysis.}{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sb180\sa180\s22\itap0{\s22\f0\fs28\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 3.3 Cycle Verification}{\s22\f0\fs28\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\sa240\itap0{\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch The complete cycle for n = 0 to 24:}{\*\abitableprops table-column-props:93.600000pt/93.600000pt/93.600000pt/93.600000pt/93.600000pt/; bot-style:1; bot-thickness:1; left-style:1; left-thickness:1; right-style:1; right-thickness:1; top-style:1; top-thickness:1; table-sdh:0x558f6e7d09c0}\par{\*\abicellprops left-attach:0; right-attach:1; top-attach:0; bot-attach:1; bot-style:1; bot-thickness:0.500000pt; left-style:1; left-thickness:0.500000pt; right-style:1; right-thickness:0.500000pt; top-style:1; top-thickness:0.500000pt; background-color:0066cc}{
\trowd \itap1\trgaph36\trql\trrh0\trleft0\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clcbpat3\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cellx1907\clvertalt\cltxlrtb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clcbpat3\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cellx3779\clvertalt\cltxlrtb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clcbpat3\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cellx5651\clvertalt\cltxlrtb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clcbpat3\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cellx7523\clvertalt\cltxlrtb\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clcbpat3\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cellx9395
\pard\plain\ltrpar\qc\intbl\itap1{\cf1\cb3\highlight3\f0\fs24\b\lang1033{\*\listtag0}n}\cell{\*\abiendcell}{\*\abicellprops left-attach:1; right-attach:2; top-attach:0; bot-attach:1; bot-style:1; bot-thickness:0.500000pt; left-style:1; left-thickness:0.500000pt; right-style:1; right-thickness:0.500000pt; top-style:1; top-thickness:0.500000pt; background-color:0066cc}
